---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import FaqBlock from '../../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../site';

const rows = [
  { metric: 'Typical transit', sea: '28–45 days (Cape-routed)', rail: '18–22 days' },
  { metric: 'Cost per 40ft', sea: '≈ $1,600 – $6,800', rail: '≈ $5,000 – $7,000+', confidence: 'LOW' },
  { metric: 'Cost relative to sea', sea: 'Baseline', rail: '2–3× sea freight' },
  { metric: 'Main risk', sea: 'Red Sea / Cape rerouting', rail: 'Russia transit + border congestion' },
  { metric: 'Best for', sea: 'Low-value bulk, price-sensitive cargo', rail: 'High-value, deadline-sensitive cargo' },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is rail faster than sea freight from China to Europe?',
    a: 'Yes — rail runs roughly 18–22 days versus 28–45 days for a Cape-routed ocean sailing. Rail\u2019s advantage has grown since 2024, when Red Sea rerouting stretched ocean transit by 10–14 days.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is cheaper, sea or rail?',
    a: 'Sea is almost always cheaper per container — roughly half to a third of rail. Rail only wins on total cost when the value of faster delivery (inventory financing, deadlines) exceeds the freight premium.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should I choose rail over sea?',
    a: 'When your cargo is high-value, deadline-sensitive or expensive to hold in transit. Rail buys back about three weeks of transit, which for electronics, auto parts or fashion often justifies the premium.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does rail avoid the Red Sea risk entirely?',
    a: 'Yes. Rail runs overland through Central Asia and Russia, so it is completely insulated from Red Sea routing and the Cape detour — the main reason it looked attractive through 2024–2026.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    type: 'FAQPage',
    data: {
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sea vs Rail Freight: China to Europe — Which to Choose"
  description="Sea freight versus New Silk Road rail for China-to-Europe shipping: transit time, cost, risk and which cargo each method suits."
  canonical={`${SITE_URL}/europe/sea-vs-rail/`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Europe', href: '/europe/' },
          { label: 'Sea vs Rail' },
        ]}
      />
      <h1>Sea vs rail: China to Europe</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The choice is not between cheap and fast — it is between slow-and-cheap
        and faster-and-dearer, and the tiebreaker is the cost of your money
        tied up in transit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <blockquote class="tldr">
      <p>
        <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Sea freight is cheaper and slower; rail is
        faster and dearer. Since the Red Sea crisis forced ocean around the Cape,
        the transit gap widened to roughly 18–22 days (rail) versus 28–45 days
        (sea). Choose rail when the value of three weeks of faster delivery
        outweighs a 2–3× freight premium.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Side by side</h2>
    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'metric', label: 'Dimension' },
        { key: 'sea', label: 'Sea freight' },
        { key: 'rail', label: 'Rail freight' },
      ]}
      rows={rows}
      caption="Indicative comparison — verify live figures before booking."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The transit gap has widened</h2>
    <p>
      For years the sea-versus-rail decision was roughly 30 days against 18.
      The Red Sea crisis changed that. Since 2024, most carriers route around
      the Cape of Good Hope to avoid the Houthi threat, adding 10–14 days to
      every China-to-Europe sailing. Ocean transit now runs 28–45 days while
      rail holds at 18–22. The gap nearly doubled — which is precisely why rail
      volumes and prices held up through the crisis even as the underlying
      economics of ocean stayed cheaper.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Cost is not the real question</h2>
    <p>
      On the freight line item, sea wins almost every time — a Cape-routed 40ft
      runs roughly $1,600–6,800, while rail sits around $5,000–7,000 or more.
      But the freight rate is only part of the landed cost. A container at sea
      for 40 days ties up the value of its cargo for a month longer than rail
      does. For high-value goods, the financing cost of that extra month —
      plus the risk of missing a season or a deadline — frequently exceeds the
      rail surcharge.
    </p>
    <p>
      The honest framing: <strong>sea wins on freight cost, rail wins on total
      cost of time.</strong> Low-value, price-sensitive cargo belongs on a
      ship. High-value, deadline-sensitive cargo usually belongs on a train —
      and the decision turns on your cost of capital, not the published rate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Risk is different, not just different in size</h2>
    <p>
      Sea freight's dominant risk today is geopolitical routing — the Red Sea,
      the Cape detour, and the war-risk premiums that feed into rates. Rail's
      dominant risk is different: the dominant corridors transit Russia, which
      carries its own insurance, compliance and reputational exposure. Neither
      is "safe"; they are two different risks. Some shippers accept Russia
      transit to keep rail's speed; others move back to ocean or onto the
      slower southern Caspian corridor. Match the risk to your own compliance
      posture, not just the price.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <FaqBlock items={faqs} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <Cta />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
